Q: How to delete all constructors of a class and prevent aggregate initialization? A: <p>In order to block aggregate initialization, you need to make your class not an <a href="https://cppreference.com/cpp/language/aggregate_initialization#Aggregate" rel="nofollow noreferrer">aggregate</a>. There are several ways to do this.</p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Constructors:</strong> Your class has constructors that are user-declared but not user-provided. This allows your class to be an aggregate prior to C++20. So one option is to compile for C++20. Another option is to change one (or more) constructors from &quot;deleted&quot; to user-provided and private (for example, change <code>mylambda() = delete;</code> to <code>mylambda();</code> add move it to a <code>private</code> section; no need to actually provide a definition). The downside is that this syntax does not match your intent, so I do not recommend it.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Data members:</strong> Your class has no data (yet). As soon as you add a protected or private (non-static) data member, your class will no longer be an aggregate. If the plan for your class calls for private (or protected) data at some point, your worry is premature. Otherwise, you could add a dummy data member, with the downside that your class will no longer be empty.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Base classes:</strong> The existence of a private, protected, or virtual base class would prevent aggregate initialization. (Prior to C++17, any base class would do, even public.) You probably do not want to introduce the complexity of virtual inheritance just to prevent aggregate initialization, but an empty base class is a reasonable option (<a href="https://cppreference.com/cpp/language/ebo" rel="nofollow noreferrer">empty bases need not increase object size</a>).</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Virtual member functions:</strong> The existence of a virtual member function would prevent aggregate initialization. However, as with virtual inheritance, you probably do not want to introduce this complexity just to prevent aggregrate initialization. On the other hand, if the plan for this class calls for virtual functions at some point, then your worry is premature.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>In summary, revisit the full design of your class. If you intend to add <strong>virtual functions</strong> or non-public, non-static <strong>data members</strong> at some point, then this issue will resolve itself at that point. (The same goes for some base classes, but if it were me, I would have already implemented the planned base classes at this point in development, so I consider this possibility unlikely.) Otherwise, I think the best option is to add an empty, private base class.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>struct empty {};

struct mylambda : private empty
{
    mylambda() = delete;
};

int main()
{
    mylambda l3;    // &lt;--- error: use of deleted function 'mylambda::mylambda()'
    mylambda l4{};  // &lt;--- error: use of deleted function 'mylambda::mylambda()'
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to refresh a div without reloading the entire page? A: <pre><code>&lt;script&gt;
$(document).ready(function () {
    // will call refreshPartial every 5 seconds
    setInterval(refreshPartial, 5000)

});

// calls action refreshing the partial
function refreshPartial() {
  $.ajax({
    url: "page_url",
    dataType: 'script',
    format: 'js'
 })
}
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: log4j no appender could be found for logger configured in standalone.xml A: <p>yo have to define an appender in your config:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;appender name="springFileAppender" class="org.apache.log4j.DailyRollingFileAppender"&gt;
    &lt;param name="File" value="../logs/spring.log" /&gt;
    &lt;param name="datePattern" value="yyyy-MM-dd" /&gt;
    &lt;layout class="org.apache.log4j.PatternLayout"&gt;
        &lt;param name="ConversionPattern" value="%d{dd-MM-yyyy kk:mm:ss.SSS} %-5p %c %x - %m%n" /&gt;

    &lt;/layout&gt;
&lt;/appender&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>then reference it from the logger :</p>

<pre><code>&lt;logger name="org.springframework.beans" additivity="true"&gt;
    &lt;level value="info" /&gt;
    &lt;appender-ref ref="springFileAppender" /&gt;
&lt;/logger&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>this config would log everything from Spring Beans module with a threshold of INFO in a file ../logs/spring.log</p>

<p>EDIT:  I see, you are using log4J with JBOSS! </p>

<p>then you should define the handler (like the appender in my example).</p>

<p>Something similar to this: </p>

<pre><code> &lt;periodic-rotating-file-handler name="FILE"&gt;
            &lt;level name="DEBUG"/&gt;
            &lt;formatter&gt;
                &lt;pattern-formatter pattern="%d{HH:mm:ss,SSS} %-5p [%c] (%t) %s%E%n"/&gt;
            &lt;/formatter&gt;
            &lt;file relative-to="jboss.server.log.dir" path="server.log"/&gt;
            &lt;suffix value=".yyyy-MM-dd"/&gt;
            &lt;append value="false"/&gt;
    &lt;/periodic-rotating-file-handler&gt;
</code></pre>
